      "writtenOn": "2026-10-24T18:20:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done: added ENABLE_X86_ASM (default ON; I went with the existing ENABLE_* naming rather than AOM_ENABLE_X86_ASM, happy to rename). With -DENABLE_X86_ASM\u003d0 on x86, HAVE_X86_ASM is 0, the .asm libraries are skipped, and the asm-only specializations drop out of RTCD. That needed a few more cases than wasm32 did: the 64-bit-only aom_dsp asm (quantize_b, fdct8x8, ssim_parms) and AVX2 functions that call SSE2/SSSE3 asm helpers (aom_convolve8_{horiz,vert}_avx2 and aom_highbd_convolve8_{horiz,vert}_avx2 are now gated, while aom_subtract_block_avx2 and the 8-wide aom_highbd_10_sub_pixel_variance avx2 functions fall back to C), plus a few test guards. Checked on x86_64: a full build with ENABLE_X86_ASM\u003d0 is clean and 5620 SSE2/SSSE3/SSE4_1/AVX2 tests pass (excluding tests that need test data); with the default ENABLE_X86_ASM\u003d1 the generated rtcd headers are byte-identical to main. nasm/yasm is still required on x86 when the option is off; I left the assembler detection and aom_ports/float.asm (Win64/Xcode) alone.",
